{
  "probe": "P-124",
  "measuredOn": "macOS 26.5.2 - arm64 - Xcode 26.2 - NLLanguageRecognizer",
  "rig": "The same zero-grant FocusNLProbe.app; two launches produced identical confidences to every printed digit, and identical to the CLI's.",
  "note": "42 labelled strings: 16 judgeable English sentences (real priorities and real window titles), 12 sub-judgeable English fragments and 14 non-English strings. Confidence is read for ENGLISH specifically rather than for the dominant language, by asking NLLanguageRecognizer for as many hypotheses as there are languages P-111 measured NLEmbedding over. MEASURED NEGATIVE RESULT, recorded rather than smoothed: the two classes OVERLAP. The lowest judgeable sentence scores 0.7618 while the fragments 'work' (0.8832), 'the thing' (0.8435) and 'catch up' (0.9090) score higher, so P-115's suggestion that this signal also separates short input from long does not generalise from its single measured point. The non-English class does separate cleanly: every one of the fourteen scores English at or below 0.0001. All three fragments above the derived floor carry fewer than minimumPriorityContentTokens content tokens, so they are declined by R-56.1's first condition instead - the two guards are complementary, which is why the overlap costs nothing.",
  "labels": {
    "judgeable": "a multi-word English sentence the sentence embedding has something to work with",
    "fragment": "an English fragment too short to be judged - P-115's measured 0.41 case and its neighbours",
    "nonEnglish": "a string for which P-111 measured NLEmbedding returning nil, so there is nothing to consult at any confidence"
  },
  "derivation": "languageConfidenceFloor = the midpoint between the LOWEST scoring judgeable English string and the HIGHEST scoring non-judgeable one (fragments and non-English together), rounded UP to two decimals (R-58, R-74.2).",
